<BaseLayout title="Help understanding your council tax bill" description="Check your band, understand the parts of a bill and find official help with discounts, reductions and payments." path="/help/">
<header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Practical next steps</p><h1>Understand your bill</h1><p class="lede">Start with the property, then the household, then the payment schedule.</p></header>
<div class="prose">
<h2>1. Check the property band</h2><p>Your band is a valuation category, not the current market price of your home. Find the recorded band through <a href="https://www.gov.uk/council-tax-bands">GOV.UK for England and Wales</a> or <a href="https://www.saa.gov.uk/">the Scottish Assessors Association</a>. Follow the official challenge process if you think it is wrong.</p>
<h2>2. Check what the quoted charge includes</h2><p>English figures on this site include the shares of the bodies charging council tax in an area, with parish precepts averaged. Scottish figures exclude water and sewerage. Welsh Band D figures include average community and police charges. Your exact bill can differ because of location, discounts or premiums. <a href="/coverage/">Read the coverage guide</a>.</p>
<h2>3. Check discounts and reductions</h2><p>A household with only one counted adult may qualify for a 25% discount. Some people are disregarded when counting adults. Check the <a href="https://www.gov.uk/council-tax/who-has-to-pay">official eligibility guidance</a> and apply through your council.</p><p>Separate support may apply to low-income households, students or disabled residents. These are different schemes with different conditions; this site does not decide eligibility.</p>
<ul><li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/apply-council-tax-reduction">Council Tax Reduction: find your local scheme</a></li><li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/council-tax/discounts-for-full-time-students">Full-time student discounts and exemptions</a></li><li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/council-tax/discounts-for-disabled-people">Disabled band reduction and other disability-related help</a></li><li><a href="https://www.mygov.scot/council-tax">Council tax guidance in Scotland</a></li><li><a href="https://www.gov.wales/council-tax">Council tax guidance in Wales</a></li></ul>
<h2>4. Check the payment schedule</h2><p>The annual charge and an instalment are different things. Our monthly equivalents are arithmetic illustrations, not a repayment schedule. Check the due dates on your bill and <a href="https://www.gov.uk/council-tax/paying-your-bill">official payment guidance</a>.</p>
<h2>5. If paying is difficult</h2><p>Contact your billing council promptly to discuss support and payments. Use <a href="https://www.gov.uk/find-local-council">the official council finder</a>. Do not send personal bills or financial details to this site.</p>
<h2>Sources and saved views</h2><p>Use the <a href="/data/">Sources catalogue</a> to open the original official publications. Compiled datasets and table CSV downloads are not provided. Council tables can be printed and supported views can be shared without a postcode.</p>
<h2>Northern Ireland</h2><p>Northern Ireland uses domestic rates. Start with <a href="https://www.nidirect.gov.uk/articles/how-rate-bills-are-calculated">nidirect's rate-bill guide</a>.</p>
<p class="source-line">Official guidance links reviewed 5 October 2026. Rules and local schemes can change; check the linked service before applying.</p>
</div>
</BaseLayout>
